  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">De stad op de berg is an open-access scholarly book with early but real signs of reach: it is available through open repository channels, is listed in library and reader-facing systems, and has generated a small amount of public trace activity. The record also shows two Crossref-linked mentions, suggesting some public discussion, while the citation count in OpenAlex remains at zero.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Jo Tollebeek and Liesbet Nys\u2019s De stad op de berg, published by Leuven University Press in 2021, sits firmly in the humanities, with art also among its strongest topical signals. The book is openly accessible through repository infrastructure, and its presence in DOAB further supports discoverability for readers and libraries interested in scholarly monographs with open access availability. That combination of publisher profile and open distribution gives the work a clear route into academic and public use.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signals in the record are not citation-based but circulation-based. The book is visible in commercial and reader-oriented platforms such as Amazon and Goodreads, and it also appears in LibraryThing, where it has a recorded reader rating. Those traces do not prove scholarly uptake on their own, but they do show that the title has entered wider reading ecosystems beyond the publisher\u2019s catalogue.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a modest sign of public discourse: Crossref records two mentions associated with the work, indicating some traceable discussion or referencing in the broader publication environment. The public-discourse record is otherwise sparse, so the safest reading is that attention exists but is limited in the currently aggregated sources.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">By contrast, citation evidence is still minimal in the available record, with OpenAlex showing zero citations at present. That means the book\u2019s scholarly footprint has not yet registered in the citation layer captured here, even though the surrounding signals point to discoverability, reader engagement, and open availability. In short, the record suggests a book that is visible and accessible, with early signs of wider circulation, while its formal citation impact is not yet established in this dataset.</p>",
